msgstr "Principios de diseño de las representaciones de operadores {#design-principles-of-operator-representations}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This guide explains the common design principles and core concepts shared across all operator representations in the [`operators`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators#module-qiskit_fermions.operators \"qiskit_fermions.operators\") module.\n"
msgstr "Esta guía explica los principios de diseño comunes y los conceptos básicos compartidos por todas las representaciones de operadores del módulo [`operators`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators#module-qiskit_fermions.operators \"qiskit_fermions.operators\").\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Overview {#overview}"
msgstr "Descripción general {#overview}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The operator representations provided by this module share several fundamental design principles:\n"
msgstr "Las representaciones de operadores que ofrece este módulo comparten varios principios de diseño fundamentales:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, no-wrap
msgid "[**Sparse data structure**](#sparse-term-representation): Operators encode only non-identity operations. The internal data layout is generally inspired by sparse matrix data formats, enabling efficient storage and computation for systems with many modes but relatively few significant contributions.\n"
msgstr "[**Estructura de datos dispersa**](#sparse-term-representation): los operadores codifican solo las operaciones distintas de la identidad. La disposición interna de los datos se inspira, en general, en los formatos de datos de matrices dispersas, lo que permite un almacenamiento y un cálculo eficientes en sistemas con muchos modos pero relativamente pocas contribuciones significativas.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, no-wrap
msgid "[**Term iteration and reconstruction**](#term-iteration-and-reconstruction): Irrespective of the internal sparse storage, operators provide a consistent iteration interface, so you can inspect, filter, and transform terms without understanding the underlying data structure, then reconstruct new operators from modified terms.\n"
msgstr "[**Iteración y reconstrucción de términos**](#term-iteration-and-reconstruction): con independencia del almacenamiento disperso interno, los operadores ofrecen una interfaz de iteración coherente, de modo que puedes inspeccionar, filtrar y transformar términos sin entender la estructura de datos subyacente y luego reconstruir nuevos operadores a partir de los términos modificados.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, no-wrap
msgid "[**Mode-based indexing**](#mode-based-indexing): Operators use abstract mode indices to label fermionic degrees of freedom, enabling flexible mapping from physical systems to the operator representation.\n"
msgstr "[**Indexación basada en modos**](#mode-based-indexing): los operadores usan índices de modo abstractos para etiquetar los grados de libertad fermiónicos, lo que permite una asignación flexible desde los sistemas físicos a la representación del operador.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, no-wrap
msgid "[**Term grouping and commutation relationships**](#term-grouping): Operators natively support grouping information that associates terms with group indices. This enables optimizations and physical structure preservation without requiring separate data structures. See the [grouping guide](grouping#grouping-explanation) for practical usage.\n"
msgstr "[**Agrupación de términos y relaciones de conmutación**](#term-grouping): los operadores admiten de forma nativa información de agrupación que asocia términos con índices de grupo. Esto permite optimizaciones y la preservación de la estructura física sin necesidad de estructuras de datos independientes. Consulta la [guía de agrupación](grouping#grouping-explanation) para ver su uso práctico.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, no-wrap
msgid "[**Arithmetic and mathematical operations**](#arithmetic-and-mathematical-operations): All operators implement a consistent set of arithmetic operations (such as addition, multiplication, and composition) and mathematical functions by using the [`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\") protocol, enabling uniform code across different operator types.\n"
msgstr "[**Operaciones aritméticas y matemáticas**](#arithmetic-and-mathematical-operations): todos los operadores implementan un conjunto coherente de operaciones aritméticas (como suma, multiplicación y composición) y de funciones matemáticas mediante el protocolo [`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\"), lo que permite un código uniforme para distintos tipos de operadores.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, no-wrap
msgid "[**Operator term ordering and normal forms**](#operator-term-ordering): Mathematically equivalent operators can have very different representations and behavior in quantum algorithms. All operator representations support various normal forms (based on algebra-specific commutation relations) to achieve canonical, predictable, and optimizable operator representations.\n"
msgstr "[**Orden de términos del operador y formas normales**](#operator-term-ordering): operadores matemáticamente equivalentes pueden tener representaciones y comportamientos muy distintos en los algoritmos cuánticos. Todas las representaciones de operadores admiten varias formas normales (basadas en relaciones de conmutación propias de cada álgebra) para lograr representaciones de operadores canónicas, predecibles y optimizables.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"sparse-term-representation\" />\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sparse data structure {#sparse-data-structure}"
msgstr "Estructura de datos dispersa {#sparse-data-structure}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "All operators use a **sparse representation** where only non-identity operations are tracked. Each non-identity operation consists of a **coefficient** (complex number) and a **sequence of actions** on specific modes.\n"
msgstr "Todos los operadores usan una **representación dispersa** en la que solo se registran las operaciones distintas de la identidad. Cada operación distinta de la identidad consta de un **coeficiente** (número complejo) y una **secuencia de acciones** sobre modos concretos.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This approach dramatically reduces memory usage and computation time, especially for systems with many modes but relatively few significant contributions. By encoding only non-identity operations, operations focus only on what matters, enabling work with large systems that would be infeasible with dense representations. Additionally, operators naturally scale to any number of modes; an operator acting on modes `{0, 1}` works unchanged in systems with many more modes since unaffected modes are implicitly identity.\n"
msgstr "Este enfoque reduce drásticamente el uso de memoria y el tiempo de cálculo, especialmente en sistemas con muchos modos pero relativamente pocas contribuciones significativas. Al codificar solo las operaciones distintas de la identidad, las operaciones se centran únicamente en lo que importa, lo que permite trabajar con sistemas grandes que serían inviables con representaciones densas. Además, los operadores escalan de forma natural a cualquier número de modos; un operador que actúa sobre los modos `{0, 1}` funciona sin cambios en sistemas con muchos más modos, ya que los modos no afectados son implícitamente la identidad.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Identical terms are preserved separately during arithmetic operations and must be explicitly combined if needed.\n"
msgstr "Los términos idénticos se conservan por separado durante las operaciones aritméticas y deben combinarse explícitamente si hace falta.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Internal storage format {#internal-storage-format}"
msgstr "Formato de almacenamiento interno {#internal-storage-format}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Internally, operators are stored in arrays inspired by sparse matrix data formats:\n"
msgstr "Internamente, los operadores se almacenan en arreglos inspirados en los formatos de datos de matrices dispersas:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Coefficients array**: The complex coefficient for each term\n"
msgstr "**Arreglo de coeficientes**: el coeficiente complejo de cada término\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Mode indices array**: The fermionic modes that each action acts upon\n"
msgstr "**Arreglo de índices de modo**: los modos fermiónicos sobre los que actúa cada acción\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Boundaries array**: Indices marking where each term’s modes begin and end in the mode array\n"
msgstr "**Arreglo de límites**: índices que marcan dónde empieza y termina en el arreglo de modos cada término\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  Additional arrays might be present depending on the operator type. For example, [`FermionOperator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-fermion-operator#qiskit_fermions.operators.FermionOperator \"qiskit_fermions.operators.FermionOperator\") instances include an **actions array** of booleans that specifies the type of fermionic action acting on the respective mode index. In contrast, the `MajoranaOperator` class does not require this distinction since it encodes that information in the parity of the mode index. See the API documentation for your operator type to understand the full storage format.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition title=\"Importante\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  Según el tipo de operador, puede haber arreglos adicionales. Por ejemplo, las instancias de [`FermionOperator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-fermion-operator#qiskit_fermions.operators.FermionOperator \"qiskit_fermions.operators.FermionOperator\") incluyen un **arreglo de acciones** de valores booleanos que especifica el tipo de acción fermiónica que actúa sobre el índice de modo correspondiente. En cambio, la clase `MajoranaOperator` no requiere esta distinción, ya que codifica esa información en la paridad del índice de modo. Consulta la documentación de la API de tu tipo de operador para conocer el formato de almacenamiento completo.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The following examples show how these arrays are organized. First is a direct construction using the sparse arrays:\n"
msgstr "Los siguientes ejemplos muestran cómo se organizan estos arreglos. Primero, una construcción directa usando los arreglos dispersos:\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Tabs group=\"code\">\n"
"  <TabItem value=\"python\" label=\"PYTHON\">\n"
msgstr ""
"<Tabs group=\"code\">\n"
"  <TabItem value=\"python\" label=\"PYTHON\">\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```python\n"
"    >>> from qiskit_fermions.operators import FermionOperator\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Construct operators directly using sparse arrays\n"
"    >>> # First operator: 1.0 * +0 -1\n"
"    >>> op1 = FermionOperator(\n"
"    ...     coeffs=[1.0],\n"
"    ...     actions=[True, False],\n"
"    ...     modes=[0, 1],\n"
"    ...     boundaries=[0, 2],\n"
"    ... )\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Second operator: 1.0 * +2 -3\n"
"    >>> op2 = FermionOperator(\n"
"    ...     coeffs=[1.0],\n"
"    ...     actions=[True, False],\n"
"    ...     modes=[2, 3],\n"
"    ...     boundaries=[0, 2],\n"
"    ... )\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Combine the sparse operators\n"
"    >>> op1 += op2\n"
"    >>> print(format(op1))\n"
"      1.000000e0 +0.000000e0j * (+0 -1)\n"
"      1.000000e0 +0.000000e0j * (+2 -3)\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "  <TabItem value=\"c\" label=\"C\">\n"
msgstr "  <TabItem value=\"c\" label=\"C\">\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```c\n"
"    #include <qiskit_fermions.h>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Construct first operator: 1.0 * +0 -1\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeff1[1] = {{1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    bool actions1[2] = {true, false};\n"
"    uint32_t modes1[2] = {0, 1};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries1[2] = {0, 2};\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *op1 = qf_ferm_op_new(1, 2, coeff1, actions1, modes1, boundaries1);\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Construct first operator: 1.0 * +0 -1\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeff1[1] = {{1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    bool actions1[2] = {true, false};\n"
"    uint32_t modes1[2] = {0, 1};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries1[2] = {0, 2};\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *op1 = qf_ferm_op_new(1, 2, coeff1, actions1, modes1, boundaries1);\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Construct second operator: 1.0 * +2 -3\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeff2[1] = {{1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    bool actions2[2] = {true, false};\n"
"    uint32_t modes2[2] = {2, 3};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries2[2] = {0, 2};\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *op2 = qf_ferm_op_new(1, 2, coeff2, actions2, modes2, boundaries2);\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Construct second operator: 1.0 * +2 -3\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeff2[1] = {{1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    bool actions2[2] = {true, false};\n"
"    uint32_t modes2[2] = {2, 3};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries2[2] = {0, 2};\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *op2 = qf_ferm_op_new(1, 2, coeff2, actions2, modes2, boundaries2);\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Combine the sparse operators. There is no in-place add in the C API, so\n"
"    // the sum is a new operator that the caller owns.\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *sum = qf_ferm_op_add(op1, op2);\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Combine the sparse operators. There is no in-place add in the C API, so\n"
"    // the sum is a new operator that the caller owns.\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *sum = qf_ferm_op_add(op1, op2);\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    qf_ferm_op_free(op1);\n"
"    qf_ferm_op_free(op2);\n"
"    qf_ferm_op_free(sum);\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"
msgstr ""
"    qf_ferm_op_free(op1);\n"
"    qf_ferm_op_free(op2);\n"
"    qf_ferm_op_free(sum);\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Convenient construction methods {#convenient-construction-methods}"
msgstr "Métodos de construcción prácticos {#convenient-construction-methods}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For Python developers, several convenient construction methods are available that abstract away the sparse storage details. These make it easier to build operators without worrying about managing coefficient, mode, and boundary arrays:\n"
msgstr "Para los desarrolladores de Python, hay varios métodos de construcción prácticos que abstraen los detalles del almacenamiento disperso. Facilitan la construcción de operadores sin tener que preocuparse por gestionar los arreglos de coeficientes, modos y límites:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```python\n"
"    >>> from qiskit_fermions.operators import FermionOperator, cre, ann\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Construct operators using operator algebra notation\n"
"    >>> op1 = FermionOperator.from_dict({(cre(0), ann(1)): 1.0})\n"
"    >>> op2 = FermionOperator.from_dict({(cre(2), ann(3)): 1.0})\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # The result is sparse even when combining them\n"
"    >>> op1 += op2\n"
"    >>> print(format(op1))\n"
"    1.000000e0 +0.000000e0j * (+0 -1)\n"
"    1.000000e0 +0.000000e0j * (+2 -3)\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```c\n"
"    // The C API uses direct array construction; convenience methods are not available.\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Hint\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  Individual operator implementations might support additional construction methods suited to their specific use case. Check the API documentation for your operator type to see all available construction options.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition title=\"Sugerencia\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  Las implementaciones individuales de operadores pueden admitir métodos de construcción adicionales adaptados a su caso de uso específico. Consulta la documentación de la API de tu tipo de operador para ver todas las opciones de construcción disponibles.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

